            <ArticleTitle>Increased hunger and speed of eating in obese children and adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>413-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/jpem-2013-0271</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/jpem.2014.27.issue-5-6/jpem-2013-0271/jpem-2013-0271.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This quality improvement program examined self-reported hunger, over-eating, and eating speed in obese and normal-weight children and adolescents prior to an interventional component.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Food frequency questionnaires were presented to 127 obese and 42 normal-weight patients, and perceived hunger, food intake and eating speed were rated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Obese patients reported significantly greater hunger than normal-weight patients (62.2% vs. 21.4%, p&lt;0.001) and faster eating (55.7% vs. 23.3%, p&lt;0.001). Patients reporting being &quot;always&quot; or &quot;often hungry&quot; were more than six times likely to be obese (OR=6.49, 2.86-14.73, p&lt;0.001), while rapid speed of eating yielded a four-fold increase in likelihood of obesity (OR=4.15, 1.77-9.72, p&lt;0.001). Hunger and speed of eating were also highly associated (p&lt;0.001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Increased hunger and eating speed were highly prevalent in these obese pediatric patients and may reflect abnormalities of satiety and satiation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
